#!/bin/sh
# pre-push - refuse the push unless the chain rehearsal record, ops\run-gates.ps1 and the test-auditors check all pass,
# and, while the chain queue is occupied, refuse a chain-touching push that is not the queue's head.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-09-09). ThriftyCrew's own CLAUDE.md said "ops/run-gates.ps1 is the change-time
# gate and runs on every push". It did not, and had not since 2026-08-11. gates.yml ran `on: push` to
# main plus pull_request until commit b8b5ac1c9, when GitHub-hosted Actions minutes were exhausted and
# all three workflows went `workflow_dispatch`-only together. RUNTIME-MAP recorded that for daily.yml
# and heartbeat.yml and never named gates.yml, so the CHANGE-TIME gate went quiet as collateral of a
# cost decision about scheduled jobs. No pre-push hook existed. No scheduled task calls run-gates.
#
# So for a month a push was gated exactly as much as the person pushing chose to gate it, while the
# instructions every session reads asserted otherwise. This restores the property at zero money cost.
#
# ONCE PER PUSH, NOT ONCE PER COMMIT. That is the point and it is why this is cheaper than the habit it
# replaces: a session that makes four commits and pushes once now runs the full 306 gates ONCE. On
# 2026-09-09 the gate went from 78s to 51s by dispatching into one pool instead of four, so the price
# of this hook is about 51 seconds of a manual push.
#
# WHY NOT RUN ONLY THE GATES A CHANGE CAN AFFECT. Measured, not assumed: the longest single gate is
# ~31s inside a 51s wall, and the expensive static detectors - audit-guard-contract, audit-write-only-
# reports, audit-source-control-bytes, audit-script-census - all scan the WHOLE TREE, so no file-based
# selection can skip them. A perfect selector would save about 20 seconds, in exchange for making "no
# gate matched your change" and "the gates found nothing" the same bytes. That is the shape
# .claude/rules/ops-and-gates.md warns about and how a gate goes quietly unfirable. Not worth 20s.
#
# THE EXIT CODE IS READ, NEVER THE TALLY. run-gates: 0 = passed, 1 = at least one gate failed,
# 3 = COULD NOT EVALUATE - nothing was judged, which is never the tree being clean. A 3 has SEVERAL causes and
# the refusal below reads which one from the gate's own blind= marker: discovery collapsing, no gate worker slot
# inside the wait, a push the remote will already reject, a pool that returned a different number of results than
# it dispatched, a self-test that exited 0 without its own verdict, or (since 2026-09-23) a static gate that exited 0
# while its own marker said it read zero files. A 3 is NEVER a pass and is refused here as loudly as a 1, whichever
# cause it was and even when the gate named none.
#
# IT STANDS IN FRONT OF THE AUTOMATED PUSHES TOO, deliberately, and that is worth knowing before it
# surprises somebody: the ~07:00 bot and wave-publish both push. run-gates is HERMETIC - it reads
# source and frozen fixtures, never a board - so ordinary daily data churn CANNOT fail it. An automated
# push is blocked only when the SOURCE is genuinely broken, which is exactly when it should be. The
# refusal goes to stderr and lands in the caller's log.
#
# BYPASS IS DELIBERATE AND LOUD: `git push --no-verify`. A gate with no escape hatch is a gate somebody
# uninstalls. ops\audit-hook-installed.ps1 asserts this file is live, so skipping it is a choice
# somebody made rather than a state nobody noticed.
#
# THE ORDER, CHEAPEST REFUSAL FIRST (2026-09-23, W1.1 of design/PLAN-push-derived-conflicts-2026-09-23.md):
#   1. read the ref lines (a push that only deletes refs exits 0 here);
#   2. resolve the working tree, and refuse when it, the gate or the test-auditors check is missing;
#   3. clear the repository environment;
#   4. seed a checkout with no built cards, once;
#   5. ask ops/rehearse-chain.ps1 -CheckPush for the recorded rehearsal verdict, which takes seconds;
#   5a. for a chain-touching push only, probe the chain queue with zero wait, and refuse one that is not its head while
#      it is occupied (W8.3, 2026-09-23);
#   6. run ops/run-gates.ps1;
#   7. run ops/prepush-test-auditors.ps1;
#   8. take the machine-wide push lock for the ref update only.
# Step 5 used to run AFTER step 7, inside the branch where test-auditors had passed, so a chain-touching push with no
# verdict paid the whole gate and a full test-auditors run before a record read that takes seconds refused it: the plan
# measured full test-auditors runs of 792 s and 709 s inside the lock before two such refusals. The same three checks
# must all pass exactly as before. The one thing that changed is which refusal a push failing several of them sees
# first: now the rehearsal's, which is also the one that prints the -NoRehearsal bypass line.
#
# EVERY REFUSAL ENDS WITH ONE FIXED LINE on stderr, for a machine to read rather than a person:
#   PRE-PUSH-REFUSED cause=<run-gates|test-auditors|rehearsal|chain-queue|structure> [gate=<first failing gate>]
# ops/push-main.ps1 classifies an in-lock refusal from it (the push ledger's reject_class) and prefers it to the prose
# above it, which changes whenever somebody improves a message. gate= is the first token after FAIL on run-gates' first
# "  FAIL" line, and is printed only when run-gates printed one. A push that exits 0 prints no such line.
#
# AND EVERY REFUSAL WRITES ONE PUSH-LEDGER ROW (2026-09-23, W0.6 of the same plan, section 15.5). The ledger
# (lib/push-ledger.ps1) had two writers, ops/hold-push-lock.ps1, which starts only after every check here has passed, and
# ops/push-main.ps1, so a plain push this hook refused left no row, and only 32 of 72 origin/main updates in the plan's
# window came through a push-main row at all. Just before the fixed line, say_refused starts
# ops/record-hook-refusal.ps1, which appends one `hook-refused` row: the cause, the gate, the refusing check's exit code
# and kept log, the pushing checkout, the first ref line's refs and shas, this hook's pid and blob, and whether the push
# runs under ops/push-main.ps1. BEST EFFORT, NEVER A VERDICT: the hook has already refused, so a writer that is missing or
# fails costs ONE printed line and changes no exit code. A pass writes no such row and starts no writer.
#
# Install:  powershell -File ops\install-hooks.ps1

# THE PUSH-LEDGER WRITER FOR A REFUSAL (2026-09-23, W0.6). Resolved like the push-lock holder below: the pushing
# checkout's copy, else the MAIN checkout's, because this hook is installed in the SHARED .git and most checkouts are
# older than any change to it. Main's copy resolves lib/push-ledger.ps1 from its own directory. It runs no git, so the
# repository environment this hook has not yet cleared on its first refusals cannot reach anything through it.
find_recorder() {
  rr_found=""
  if [ -n "$repo" ] && [ -f "$repo/ops/record-hook-refusal.ps1" ]; then
    rr_found="$repo/ops/record-hook-refusal.ps1"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ -n "$repo" ]; then
    rr_common="$(git -C "$repo" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)"
  else
    rr_common="$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)"
  fi
  case "$rr_common" in
    */.git)
      if [ -f "${rr_common%/.git}/ops/record-hook-refusal.ps1" ]; then
        rr_found="${rr_common%/.git}/ops/record-hook-refusal.ps1"
      fi
      ;;
  esac
}

# $1 cause, $2 gate, $3 the refusing check's exit code, $4 its kept log, $5 run-gates' blind= token. Every value that is
# empty is LEFT OUT of the writer's arguments rather than passed as "": powershell.exe -File drops an empty argument and
# binds the next token to the parameter instead (grocery/audit-script-census.ps1 measured it), which would shift every
# value after it. set -- inside a function replaces only the function's own arguments, so the copies come first.
record_refusal() {
  rr_cause="$1"; rr_gate="$2"; rr_rc="$3"; rr_log="$4"; rr_blind="$5"
  find_recorder
  if [ -z "$rr_found" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: this refusal was not recorded in the push ledger (no ops/record-hook-refusal.ps1 in this checkout or the main one); the refusal stands." >&2
    return 0
  fi
  rr_hookpid="$(cat /proc/$$/winpid 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$rr_hookpid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) rr_hookpid="" ;; esac
  rr_blob="$(git hash-object --no-filters -- "$0" 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ -n "$rr_log" ] && command -v cygpath >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    rr_log="$(cygpath -w "$rr_log")"
  fi
  set -- -Cause "$rr_cause"
  [ -n "$rr_gate" ] && set -- "$@" -Gate "$rr_gate"
  [ -n "$rr_rc" ] && set -- "$@" -Rc "$rr_rc"
  [ -n "$rr_blind" ] && set -- "$@" -Blind "$rr_blind"
  [ -n "$rr_log" ] && set -- "$@" -Log "$rr_log"
  [ -n "$repo" ] && set -- "$@" -Checkout "$repo"
  [ -n "$push_remote_name" ] && set -- "$@" -Remote "$push_remote_name"
  [ -n "$first_lref" ] && set -- "$@" -LocalRef "$first_lref"
  [ -n "$first_lsha" ] && set -- "$@" -LocalSha "$first_lsha"
  [ -n "$first_rref" ] && set -- "$@" -RemoteRef "$first_rref"
  [ -n "$first_rsha" ] && set -- "$@" -RemoteSha "$first_rsha"
  set -- "$@" -RefLines "$ref_lines"
  [ -n "$rr_hookpid" ] && set -- "$@" -HookPid "$rr_hookpid"
  [ -n "$rr_blob" ] && set -- "$@" -HookBlob "$rr_blob"
  # </dev/null: the ref loop has already read this hook's stdin, and the writer must not inherit it.
  rr_out="$(powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$rr_found" "$@" < /dev/null 2>&1)"
  rr_status=$?
  if [ "$rr_status" -ne 0 ]; then
    rr_why="$(printf '%s\n' "$rr_out" | tail -n 1)"
    echo "pre-push: this refusal was not recorded in the push ledger (${rr_why:-the writer exited $rr_status and said nothing}); the refusal stands." >&2
  fi
  return 0
}

# THE FIXED REFUSAL LINE (2026-09-23, W1.1). Every BLOCKED and REFUSING exit below calls this LAST, just before exit 1.
# printf, never echo: a gate path carries backslashes, and an echo that interprets them would rewrite it.
# The ledger row is written FIRST (W0.6), so a could-not-record line never lands after the fixed line: the fixed line
# stays the hook's last word. Arguments: cause, gate, and then record_refusal's rc, log and blind token.
say_refused() {
  record_refusal "$1" "$2" "$3" "$4" "$5"
  if [ -n "$2" ]; then
    printf 'PRE-PUSH-REFUSED cause=%s gate=%s\n' "$1" "$2" >&2
  else
    printf 'PRE-PUSH-REFUSED cause=%s\n' "$1" >&2
  fi
}

# A push that only DELETES refs introduces no new code to gate. stdin carries one line per ref as
# "<local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>", and an all-zero local sha is a deletion.
# READ BEFORE ANYTHING ASKS GIT FOR THE TREE (2026-09-11). The tree refusal below used to come first, so
# while the shared repository was bare a push that only deleted a branch was refused along with every
# other push. A deletion carries no code for the gate to read and needs no working tree to judge.
# The ref lines are kept for the test-auditors check below, which needs to know WHAT is being pushed.
refs="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tc-prepush-refs-$$.txt"
: > "$refs"
have_work=0
# THE FIRST REF LINE THAT CARRIES WORK, and how many lines git handed over, kept for a refusal's ledger row (W0.6): the
# refs file is removed before most refusals, and the row must still say what was being pushed. The remote NAME is kept
# too, never its URL ($2), which can carry a credential.
push_remote_name="$1"
first_lref=""; first_lsha=""; first_rref=""; first_rsha=""; ref_lines=0
while read -r lref lsha rref rsha; do
  [ -z "$lsha" ] && continue
  echo "$lref $lsha $rref $rsha" >> "$refs"
  ref_lines=$((ref_lines + 1))
  case "$lsha" in
    *[!0]*)
      have_work=1
      if [ -z "$first_lsha" ]; then
        first_lref="$lref"; first_lsha="$lsha"; first_rref="$rref"; first_rsha="$rsha"
      fi
      ;;
  esac
done
if [ "$have_work" -eq 0 ]; then
  rm -f "$refs"
  exit 0
fi

repo="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"
# NO WORKING TREE IS A REFUSAL, NOT A PASS (2026-09-10). This line used to `exit 0`. While the shared
# repository was turned bare by the incident described below the unset line, --show-toplevel failed in
# every checkout, and a sibling session's push at 05:28:19 went out through this hook with no gate run
# and no log. A hook that cannot find the tree it is meant to gate has verified nothing - the same rule
# this file already applies to a missing gate.
if [ -z "$repo" ]; then
  rm -f "$refs"
  echo "pre-push: REFUSING - git could not resolve this checkout's working tree, so nothing gated this push." >&2
  echo "          If 'git config core.bare' says true in a checkout, the shared config is damaged: repair that first." >&2
  echo "          Bypass with: git push --no-verify" >&2
  say_refused structure
  exit 1
fi
gate="$repo/ops/run-gates.ps1"

# A MISSING GATE MUST NOT READ AS A PASS. That is the fail-open-reads-as-empty class, and it is exactly
# how a guard stops guarding without anyone editing it. Same rule the pre-commit hook states.
if [ ! -f "$gate" ]; then
  rm -f "$refs"
  echo "pre-push: REFUSING - ops/run-gates.ps1 is missing, so nothing verified this push." >&2
  echo "          Restore it, or push with --no-verify if you have decided that is right." >&2
  say_refused structure
  exit 1
fi

ta="$repo/ops/prepush-test-auditors.ps1"
if [ ! -f "$ta" ]; then
  rm -f "$refs"
  echo "pre-push: REFUSING - ops/prepush-test-auditors.ps1 is missing, so nothing can say whether this push blinds a guard." >&2
  echo "          Restore it, or push with --no-verify if you have decided that is right." >&2
  say_refused structure
  exit 1
fi

# THE GATE RUNS WITH NO REPOSITORY ENVIRONMENT (2026-09-10). A push from a LINKED worktree makes git
# export GIT_DIR=<common .git>/worktrees/<name> to this hook; a push from the main checkout exports
# none, which is why nobody had seen this. run-gates inherited it, and so did its hermetic git
# self-tests: their temp-repo `git init` and `git config user.name` landed on the SHARED repository.
# Measured on the first push from a detached gate-check checkout: core.bare=true, user.name=Session,
# user.email=t@t, core.autocrlf=false and commit.gpgsign=false written into the common .git/config,
# and every checkout on the box then refused `git status` until it was repaired by hand. The gate
# needs none of these - run-gates finds the repo from its own path - and `repo` above is already
# resolved. ops/test-prepush-hook.ps1 drives this hook from a linked worktree in a sandbox.
unset GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE GIT_INDEX_FILE GIT_COMMON_DIR GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES GIT_PREFIX GIT_NAMESPACE

# SEED A BLIND CHECKOUT ONCE, BEFORE THE GATE (2026-09-11). meal-prep/db/built is gitignored and
# .worktreeinclude structurally CANNOT carry it: Claude Code's candidate list is built with
# `git ls-files --directory`, which collapses a fully-ignored directory to ONE line - measured that day as
# 1 line against 1,168 files on disk. So a fresh worktree has no built card, and feed-covers-published and
# wave-preaudit could not look. Both now report that BLIND rather than FAIL, which stops the refusal; this
# makes them actually LOOK, which is the half that restores the coverage. Measured 2026-09-11: 31 of 106
# worktrees had no card, and 18 of 53 failed gate runs that day failed on nothing else
# (design/PLAN-gate-queue-2026-09-11.md).
#
# BEST EFFORT, NEVER A VERDICT. Seeding is an enabler, not a check, so a seed that cannot run must not
# refuse the push - the gates then report BLIND, which is the honest outcome and is printed. That is why
# this does not follow the "a missing gate is a refusal" rule the blocks above apply: those decide, this
# one only supplies. It runs at most once per checkout, because the guard below sees the seeded card.
seedcard="$repo/meal-prep/db/built/american-goulash-pasta.body.html"
seeder="$repo/ops/seed-worktree.ps1"
if [ ! -f "$seedcard" ] && [ -f "$seeder" ]; then
  echo "pre-push: this checkout has no built cards, so two gates would be BLIND - seeding once (47 MB)." >&2
  powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$seeder" -Target "$repo" >&2 || \
    echo "pre-push: seeding did not complete; the gates will report BLIND rather than fail." >&2
fi

# ONE PUSH AT A TIME ON THIS BOX (2026-09-11, design/PLAN-push-livelock-2026-09-11.md).
# git fixes a push's refs when it connects, and the remote updates a ref only if it STILL holds the sha this hook was
# handed. So a push is a compare-and-swap whose critical section is this whole hook. Measured that day across 11
# consecutive attempts from one session: 577 to 1,840 s each, run-gates PASSED every time, and every single one was
# rejected with "cannot lock ref 'refs/heads/main' ... is at X but expected Y" while other sessions landed every 15 to
# 25 minutes. That is the starvation of the longest transaction under optimistic concurrency control, and the slowest
# push converges on never landing however green its gates are. The retry is not free either: the session must rebase,
# a rebase is genuinely new content, so the recorded verdict correctly does not apply and all 341 to 368 gates run
# again. Eleven attempts, eleven full gates, nothing landed.
#
# SERIALISING COSTS NO THROUGHPUT. refs/heads/main is ALREADY serialised - one push lands at a time whatever anyone
# does - so today's parallelism is not parallel landing, it is parallel GATING of which all but one result is thrown
# away. ops/probe-push-throughput.ps1 is the harness that holds that claim to account, and its numbers are in
# design/MEASURE-push-lock-2026-09-11.md.
#
# UNLOCKED IS NOT A REFUSAL. This is a FAIRNESS device; the gate is what makes a push safe, and nothing in the lock
# reads a tree. This file is installed in the SHARED .git and stands in front of every checkout on this box, most of
# them older than this change, so every lock path degrades to the behaviour of the day before it existed: no holder
# script in the pushing checkout, a wedged queue, or anything thrown, and the push simply races for the ref as it
# always did. It is said out loud either way.
lockdir=""
release_push_lock() {
  if [ -n "$lockdir" ]; then
    : > "$lockdir/release" 2>/dev/null
    # The directory goes too: one per push would otherwise accumulate in %TEMP% forever, and the estate's
    # test-suites-leak-temp-dirs rule puts that on whoever allocated it. This does NOT wait for the holder to
    # notice - a `wait` here could block the hook's exit if the holder ever hung - so removing the release file
    # out from under it falls back to its SECOND end condition, the watched hook process disappearing, which is
    # about to happen anyway and is checked every 250 ms.
    rm -rf "$lockdir" 2>/dev/null
    lockdir=""
  fi
}
# EVERY EXIT PATH RELEASES, including the ones below this line that were written before the lock existed and the ones
# a future change adds. A trap is the only way to get that property without editing each of them.
trap release_push_lock EXIT
holder="$repo/ops/hold-push-lock.ps1"
# AND WHEN THIS CHECKOUT IS OLDER, THE MAIN ONE'S COPY (2026-09-12). The line above resolves the holder from the
# PUSHING checkout, so the lock reached only the worktrees that had rebased since it shipped - and a fairness device
# that reaches some of the queue is worse than none: the sessions that updated queue politely while the ones that did
# not sail past them to the ref. MEASURED that morning: 30 of 132 worktrees carried this script, 9 pushes were queued
# on the lock with the oldest waiting 13 minutes, and pushes from the other 102 were landing straight past them.
# The hook itself is installed in the SHARED .git and is always current, so it can reach the main checkout's copy,
# which is current too. hold-push-lock resolves lib\push-lock.ps1 from its OWN directory, so main's copy runs against
# main's library rather than an older worktree's.
# STILL NEVER A REFUSAL. If git cannot name the main checkout, or it has no copy either, $holder simply stays missing
# and this push races for the ref exactly as it did the day before the lock existed.
if [ ! -f "$holder" ]; then
  common="$(git -C "$repo" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$common" in
    */.git)
      main_checkout="${common%/.git}"
      if [ -f "$main_checkout/ops/hold-push-lock.ps1" ]; then
        holder="$main_checkout/ops/hold-push-lock.ps1"
        echo "pre-push: this checkout has no push-lock holder, using the main checkout's - otherwise this push would jump the queue others are waiting in." >&2
      fi
      ;;
  esac
fi
# TAKEN AFTER THE CHECKS, NOT ACROSS THEM (2026-09-12, Brad: "We cant have pushes waiting so long for 'checks'
# before it gets pushed"). The lock was acquired here, at the top, and held across the whole gate, so every session
# in the queue waited out every other session's checks: MEASURED that morning, 9 pushes queued with the oldest at
# 13 minutes, and one of this session's own pushes waited 957s for a lock it then could not use because main had
# moved. A hold is a gate run plus the auditors, about two minutes, so twenty sessions is over half an hour.
#
# WHAT THE ORIGINAL DESIGN BOUGHT, and why this can now give it up. Holding across the gate meant only one session
# gated at a time, so no gate run was ever thrown away by losing the race (design/MEASURE-push-lock-2026-09-11.md:
# 550 gate-seconds wasted an arm against 0). That argument rested on a losing re-gate costing a FULL run. It no
# longer does: lib/gate-input-key.ps1 keys each self-test on what it reads, so after a rebase the self-tests whose
# inputs did not move are reused and the re-gate is a fraction of a run. The waste it prevented got cheap; the
# queue it created did not.
#
# SO THE CRITICAL SECTION IS THE PUSH ITSELF. The holder watches the PARENT - git - rather than this hook, because
# the ref update happens after this hook exits, and a lock released at hook exit never covered it. git finishing
# ends the hold; the holder's own cap ends it if git hangs.
take_push_lock() {
  [ -f "$holder" ] || return 0
  lockdir="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tc-pushlock-$$"
  rm -rf "$lockdir"; mkdir -p "$lockdir"
  lockdir_win="$lockdir"
  if command -v cygpath >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    lockdir_win="$(cygpath -w "$lockdir")"
  fi
  # THE WINDOWS PID, NEVER `$$` (2026-09-11, and this silently disabled the whole lock in its first version).
  # The holder releases as soon as the hook it watches is gone, and it asks Get-Process. Git Bash's `$$` is an MSYS
  # pid from a DIFFERENT namespace: measured here, `$$` was 172200 while the real Windows pid was 135392, and
  # `Get-Process -Id 172200` threw ProcessCommandException. Test-TcProcessAlive reads a thrown
  # ProcessCommandException as "gone" - which is the right default for a pid it cannot find - so the holder decided
  # the hook was dead about 250 ms after taking the lock, and handed it straight back. Every sandbox case still
  # passed, because none of them could see WHEN the lock was released; the gate stub now takes that reading.
  # /proc/<pid>/winpid is the MSYS translation. With no /proc the watch is simply not armed, and the release file
  # plus the hang guard end the hold - degrade, never a refusal.
  # THE PARENT'S WINDOWS PID: git's, not this hook's. The hook exits and git then updates the ref, so a hold that
  # ended with the hook ended before the thing it protects. If /proc cannot name the parent the watch falls back to
  # this hook's own pid, which is the behaviour of the day before - degrade, never a refusal.
  lockwatch="$(cat /proc/$PPID/winpid 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$lockwatch" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) lockwatch="$(cat /proc/$$/winpid 2>/dev/null)" ;;
  esac
  case "$lockwatch" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) lockwatch=0 ;;
  esac
  # </dev/null so the background child cannot inherit this hook's stdin, which the ref loop above has already read.
  powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$holder" -SignalDir "$lockdir_win" -WatchPid "$lockwatch" \
    > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null &
  lockpid=$!
  echo "pre-push: waiting for the machine-wide push lock, so this push is not beaten to the ref after it passes." >&2
  # BOUNDED BY THE HOLDER BEING ALIVE, not by a clock here: the holder's own wait is the queue's, and a holder that
  # died without writing its state must not leave this hook waiting for a file nobody will write.
  while [ ! -f "$lockdir/state" ] && kill -0 "$lockpid" 2>/dev/null; do
    sleep 1
  done
  if [ -f "$lockdir/state" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: push lock - $(head -n 1 "$lockdir/state")" >&2
  else
    echo "pre-push: the push lock holder exited without saying anything, so this push races for the ref as it did before." >&2
    release_push_lock
  fi
}

# A CHAIN-TOUCHING PUSH CARRIES A REHEARSAL VERDICT (2026-09-22, RCA F2, grocery/triage-plans/plan-2026-09-22-7.json).
# 8 of the defects of 09-20 to 09-22 passed run-gates and failed on the next real board, because nothing ran the daily
# chain over real data before the bot did. ops/rehearse-chain.ps1 -CheckPush reads git's ref lines and, for a push to
# main that changes a script in ops/chain-manifest.json, ALLOWS only on a recorded rehearsal pass over data no older than
# two days. It never rehearses: it decides from the record in seconds, BEFORE the push lock below is taken, so the
# ~14-minute rehearsal itself runs outside the lock (ops/push-main.ps1 runs it there, or run the script by hand).
# Exit 1 is "rehearsed and failed" or "no verdict"; exit 3 is "could not rehearse" and names its blind= cause; neither is
# a pass. The loud bypass is TC_NO_REHEARSAL=<reason> (push-main -NoRehearsal sets it), which is printed and logged. A
# checkout that predates the script has nothing to check with and says so, as the push-lock holder does.
#
# ASKED FIRST, BEFORE THE GATE (2026-09-23, W1.1 of design/PLAN-push-derived-conflicts-2026-09-23.md). This check used
# to run after test-auditors, and its refusal printed only when test-auditors had passed, so a chain push with no
# verdict paid run-gates and a full test-auditors run for an answer this record read gives in seconds. It now runs
# here, and a refusal exits here, before run-gates is started. $refs is written at the top of this file, so its input
# already exists; it is removed on this exit path as on every other early one, and kept on the way through because
# test-auditors reads it too. The call, its exit codes and its TC_NO_REHEARSAL handling are exactly what they were.
rh="$repo/ops/rehearse-chain.ps1"
rhrc=0
rhlog="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tc-prepush-rh-$$.log"
if [ -f "$rh" ]; then
  powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$rh" -CheckPush -RefsFromStdin < "$refs" > "$rhlog" 2>/dev/null
  rhrc=$?
  if [ "$rhrc" -eq 0 ] && ! tail -n 1 "$rhlog" | grep -q -E "^CHAIN-REHEARSAL-CHECK-COMPLETE"; then
    rhrc=4
  fi
else
  echo "pre-push: this checkout has no ops/rehearse-chain.ps1, so no chain rehearsal was asked of this push." > "$rhlog"
fi
if [ "$rhrc" -ne 0 ]; then
  rm -f "$refs"
  grep -v -E "^CHAIN-REHEARSAL-CHECK-COMPLETE" "$rhlog" >&2
  echo "" >&2
  case "$rhrc" in
    3) echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - the chain rehearsal COULD NOT EVALUATE (exit 3). A could-not-rehearse is NEVER a pass; its blind= cause is above." >&2 ;;
    4) echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - the chain rehearsal check exited 0 without its completion marker, so it decided nothing." >&2 ;;
    *) echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - this push changes the daily chain and carries no passing rehearsal over recent data (exit $rhrc)." >&2 ;;
  esac
  echo "          Rehearse:  powershell -File ops/rehearse-chain.ps1   (or land with ops/push-main.ps1, which rehearses outside the lock)" >&2
  echo "          Bypass loudly: ops/push-main.ps1 -NoRehearsal -NoRehearsalReason '<why>'  (a plain push: TC_NO_REHEARSAL='<why>')" >&2
  echo "          full output kept at: $rhlog" >&2
  say_refused rehearsal "" "$rhrc" "$rhlog"
  exit 1
fi
grep -v -E "^CHAIN-REHEARSAL-CHECK-COMPLETE" "$rhlog" >&2
# The check's own verdict of what this push IS, read off its completion marker before the log goes: not-needed means it
# touches no chain script (or goes to a branch other than main); anything else that exited 0 (rehearsed-pass, bypassed)
# is a chain-touching push. Empty when this checkout has no rehearse-chain.ps1, which asks the queue nothing below.
rh_outcome="$(tail -n 1 "$rhlog" 2>/dev/null | sed -n -E 's/^CHAIN-REHEARSAL-CHECK-COMPLETE .*outcome=([A-Za-z0-9_-]+).*$/\1/p')"
rm -f "$rhlog"

# A CHAIN PUSH THAT IS NOT THE CHAIN QUEUE'S HEAD IS REFUSED IN SECONDS WHILE THE QUEUE IS OCCUPIED (2026-09-23, W8.3 of
# design/PLAN-push-derived-conflicts-2026-09-23.md as amended in section 16.4, Brad's ruling D14). ops/push-main.ps1 puts
# every chain-touching push into a machine-wide queue (lib/chain-queue.ps1, W9.2), and each member's rehearsal is of its
# HEAD stacked on the members ahead of it, so the queue lands chain changes one at a time in ticket order and nobody's
# verdict is voided by the push ahead. A chain push that does not go through push-main joins no queue: landing past the
# members, it voids every stacked verdict behind it. Measured before the queue (15.3, SCRATCH): 6 of 17 chain landings
# after the rehearsal gate ignored the lease the queue replaces, and 1 of that day's 2 during-run voids was one of them.
#
# SO, only for a push the check above calls chain-touching, and before run-gates and before any lock: ask the queue,
# with ZERO WAIT, whether any live ticket that has not landed or left exists. If one does and this push's inherited
# TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_HOLDER does not name the HEAD of the queue (the oldest such ticket, as a live member of THIS instance,
# by Test-TcChainQueueHolderToken), refuse with cause=chain-queue. push-main exports that token for as long as its
# member holds a ticket, so its own `git push` child passes; so does a member that is the head, which is the only time
# push-main pushes at all. A queue that is free, or that cannot be read, proceeds exactly as before, with one WARN line
# naming push-main as the route for a chain change.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT SECTION 10's NEVER. That row forbids WAITING on the queue in the hook, which would add a wait-for edge
# from inside the push lock's critical section. The probe acquires nothing: Get-TcChainQueueLive and
# Test-TcChainQueueHolderToken read tickets by their mutexes without holding one, and this runs before the push lock is
# taken. Lock order 0b (plan 16.6): nothing that holds the push lock, a gate slot or a rehearsal slot waits on this.
#
# A WEDGED QUEUE IS NOT A REASON TO REFUSE. lib/chain-queue.ps1 times a waiter out when the count ahead of it has not
# fallen for $script:TcChainQueueStallSec (3600 s, read from the library, never copied), and the timed-out waiter writes
# `left`, drops its token and pushes unqueued: "a wedge costs the queue's ordering and never a push" (its header). Without
# this, that push would reach this probe with no token and a live head ahead, and be refused. So a head whose record has
# not been rewritten for longer than that bound is read as wedged, and the push proceeds with a line saying so. The
# residual, stated: a head that keeps rewriting its record (a catch-up or hand-back rebase) without ever landing is not
# wedged by this test, and its timed-out waiters are refused here until it lands or leaves.
#
# NEVER A REFUSAL IT CANNOT JUSTIFY. The library is resolved like the push-lock holder: the pushing checkout's copy, else
# the MAIN checkout's, so an older checkout is still asked (a checkout that could skip the queue is the exposure this
# closes). No copy anywhere, a probe that throws, or anything it cannot read, is a printed line and the push proceeds.
# TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_PREFIX and TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_ROOT move the probe to a private instance for fixtures, honoured only for a
# Local\ prefix, the rule TC_PUSH_LOCK_PREFIX follows.
if [ -n "$rh_outcome" ] && [ "$rh_outcome" != "not-needed" ]; then
  cq_lib=""
  if [ -f "$repo/lib/chain-queue.ps1" ]; then
    cq_lib="$repo/lib/chain-queue.ps1"
  else
    cq_common="$(git -C "$repo" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$cq_common" in
      */.git)
        if [ -f "${cq_common%/.git}/lib/chain-queue.ps1" ]; then
          cq_lib="${cq_common%/.git}/lib/chain-queue.ps1"
        fi
        ;;
    esac
  fi
  cq_warn="pre-push: WARN - this push changes the daily chain. Land chain changes with ops\\push-main.ps1, which joins the chain queue, so this push cannot void the rehearsals queued behind it."
  if [ -z "$cq_lib" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: no lib/chain-queue.ps1 in this checkout or the main one, so the chain queue was not asked; this push proceeds." >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$cq_warn" >&2
  else
    cqlog="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tc-prepush-cq-$$.log"
    # Single-quoted for sh: the probe's own text holds no single quote, and every value reaches it through the
    # environment, never through its source, so no path or token can change what it runs.
    TC_HOOK_CQ_LIB="$cq_lib" powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command '
$ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"
try {
  . $env:TC_HOOK_CQ_LIB
  $pa = @{}
  $pf = [string]$env:TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_PREFIX
  if ($pf) {
    if (-not $pf.StartsWith("Local\", [StringComparison]::Ordinal)) { throw ("TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_PREFIX is honoured only as a private Local\ name, not " + $pf) }
    $pa["Prefix"] = $pf
    if ($env:TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_ROOT) { $pa["QueueRoot"] = [string]$env:TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_ROOT }
  }
  $liveRaw = Get-TcChainQueueLive @pa
  $live = @($liveRaw | Where-Object { $null -ne $_ })
  $pending = @($live | Where-Object { @("landed", "left") -notcontains [string]$_.State })
  if ($pending.Count -eq 0) { Write-Output ("CQ-PROBE free live=" + $live.Count); exit 0 }
  $head = $pending[0]
  $tok = [string]$env:TC_CHAIN_QUEUE_HOLDER
  $tokTicket = ""
  if ($tok) { $parts = $tok.Split("|"); if ($parts.Count -ge 2) { $tokTicket = [string]$parts[1] } }
  $member = $false
  if ($tok) { $member = [bool](Test-TcChainQueueHolderToken -Token $tok @pa) }
  if ($member -and [string]::Equals($tokTicket, [string]$head.Name, [StringComparison]::Ordinal)) {
    Write-Output ("CQ-PROBE head ticket=" + $head.Name + " pending=" + $pending.Count); exit 0
  }
  $stall = [int]$script:TcChainQueueStallSec
  $age = -1
  try {
    $u = $head.Record.updated_utc
    if ($u -is [datetime]) { $ut = $u.ToUniversalTime() } else { $ut = [datetime]::Parse([string]$u, [Globalization.CultureInfo]::InvariantCulture, [Globalization.DateTimeStyles]::RoundtripKind).ToUniversalTime() }
    $age = [int][Math]::Floor(([datetime]::UtcNow - $ut).TotalSeconds)
  } catch { $age = -1 }
  if ($stall -gt 0 -and $age -gt $stall) {
    Write-Output ("CQ-PROBE wedged pid=" + $head.Pid + " checkout=" + $head.Checkout + " state=" + $head.State + " age=" + $age + " stall=" + $stall); exit 0
  }
  $why = "not-a-member"
  if ($member) { $why = "member-not-head" }
  Write-Output ("CQ-PROBE occupied why=" + $why + " pid=" + $head.Pid + " checkout=" + $head.Checkout + " state=" + $head.State + " pending=" + $pending.Count)
  exit 1
} catch {
  Write-Output ("CQ-PROBE error " + $_.Exception.Message)
  exit 3
}
' < /dev/null > "$cqlog" 2>&1
    cqrc=$?
    cqline="$(grep -E '^CQ-PROBE ' "$cqlog" 2>/dev/null | tail -n 1)"
    if [ "$cqrc" -eq 1 ] && printf '%s\n' "$cqline" | grep -q -E '^CQ-PROBE occupied '; then
      rm -f "$refs"
      cq_pid="$(printf '%s\n' "$cqline" | sed -n -E 's/.* pid=([^ ]*).*/\1/p')"
      cq_co="$(printf '%s\n' "$cqline" | sed -n -E 's/.* checkout=(.*) state=.*/\1/p')"
      cq_why="$(printf '%s\n' "$cqline" | sed -n -E 's/.* why=([^ ]*).*/\1/p')"
      echo "" >&2
      echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - a chain-touching push is landing through the chain queue (head pid ${cq_pid:-unknown}, ${cq_co:-unknown checkout})." >&2
      if [ "$cq_why" = "member-not-head" ]; then
        echo "          This push holds a queue ticket but is not the head: it must wait for the tickets ahead of it to land or leave." >&2
      fi
      echo "          Land chain changes with ops\\push-main.ps1, which joins the queue and lands in turn." >&2
      echo "          Bypass with: git push --no-verify" >&2
      echo "          probe output kept at: $cqlog" >&2
      say_refused chain-queue "" 1 "$cqlog"
      exit 1
    fi
    if [ "$cqrc" -eq 0 ] && printf '%s\n' "$cqline" | grep -q -E '^CQ-PROBE head '; then
      echo "pre-push: chain queue - this push is the queue's head, so it lands in turn." >&2
    elif [ "$cqrc" -eq 0 ] && printf '%s\n' "$cqline" | grep -q -E '^CQ-PROBE free '; then
      printf '%s\n' "$cq_warn" >&2
    elif [ "$cqrc" -eq 0 ] && printf '%s\n' "$cqline" | grep -q -E '^CQ-PROBE wedged '; then
      echo "pre-push: the chain queue's head has not moved past the queue's own stall bound (${cqline#CQ-PROBE wedged }), so the queue is wedged and this push proceeds, as its timed-out waiters do." >&2
      printf '%s\n' "$cq_warn" >&2
    else
      echo "pre-push: the chain queue could not be read (${cqline:-the probe exited $cqrc and said nothing}); this push proceeds as before." >&2
      printf '%s\n' "$cq_warn" >&2
    fi
    rm -f "$cqlog"
  fi
fi

echo "pre-push: running ops/run-gates.ps1 (about 51s) - once for this push, not once per commit." >&2

# REDIRECT, THEN READ THE STATUS, THEN GREP THE FILE. Reading a status through a pipe gives the LAST
# stage's, not the gate's, which this estate has a memory about.
log="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tc-prepush-$$.log"
# THE GATE IS TOLD WHAT THIS PUSH IS (2026-09-11). Given the refs and the remote's URL it can ask whether any of
# them still holds the sha git handed this hook, and refuse in seconds - before queueing for a gate worker slot and
# running every gate - when another push has already landed and the remote will reject this one whatever the gates
# say (lib/push-landable.ps1). The path crosses into PowerShell in Windows form; with no cygpath the gate simply
# cannot read the file and decides nothing, which is the same as a remote it cannot reach.
refs_win="$refs"
if command -v cygpath >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  refs_win="$(cygpath -w "$refs")"
fi
# ONLY TO A GATE THAT ACCEPTS THEM. This hook is installed in the SHARED .git, so it stands in front of every
# checkout on this box, and most of them are older than this change. run-gates declares [CmdletBinding()], which
# makes an undeclared argument a HARD ERROR - so handing -PushRefsFile to an older copy would refuse every push from
# every worktree until each one rebased. The flags go only to a gate whose own source names them; an older checkout
# is gated exactly as it was before.
if grep -q "PushRefsFile" "$gate" 2>/dev/null; then
  powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$gate" -PushRefsFile "$refs_win" -PushRemote "$2" > "$log" 2>/dev/null
else
  powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$gate" > "$log" 2>/dev/null
fi
rc=$?

if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
  tail -n 1 "$log" >&2
  rm -f "$log"

  # TEST-AUDITORS BEFORE A GUARD-TOUCHING PUSH (2026-09-10, design/PLAN-zero-alert-days-2026-09-10.md
  # Phase 5). run-gates is hermetic and cannot reach grocery/test-auditors.ps1, which reads the boards, so
  # a commit that broke it went out past a clean gate (queue 2026-09-10-4ac6ae), and three "a GUARD has
  # gone blind" alerts in 20 days were a triage session's own in-flight edits. The check derives the
  # suite's inputs from its source, does NOT run it for a push that touches none of them, and refuses
  # only a failing case the daily chain's known-failures record does not already hold. A checkout with
  # no boards is exit 3 and refused, because a could-not-evaluate is never a pass. The whole account,
  # and the measurements behind each choice, are in the script's header.
  talog="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tc-prepush-ta-$$.log"
  powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$ta" -RefsFromStdin < "$refs" > "$talog" 2>/dev/null
  tarc=$?
  rm -f "$refs"
  grep -v -E "^PREPUSH-TEST-AUDITORS-COMPLETE" "$talog" >&2
  # An exit 0 counts only with the completion marker as the last line: a check that died early has
  # not decided anything.
  if [ "$tarc" -eq 0 ] && tail -n 1 "$talog" | grep -q -E "^PREPUSH-TEST-AUDITORS-COMPLETE"; then
    rm -f "$talog"
    # EVERY CHECK HAS PASSED, so now take the lock and hold it across the ref update only. The trap is cleared
    # first: it exists to hand the lock back on every failing path, and on THIS path the holder must outlive the
    # hook, because git has not pushed yet. What ends the hold is git exiting, which the holder watches.
    take_push_lock
    trap - EXIT
    exit 0
  fi
  echo "" >&2
  if [ "$tarc" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - the test-auditors check exited 0 without its completion marker, so it decided nothing." >&2
  elif [ "$tarc" -eq 3 ]; then
    echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - the test-auditors check COULD NOT EVALUATE (exit 3). That is not a pass." >&2
  else
    echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - the test-auditors check exited $tarc. This push leaves a watcher that cannot see its own bug." >&2
    echo "          Fix the cause, never the guard and never the known-failures record." >&2
  fi
  echo "          full output kept at: $talog" >&2
  echo "          Bypass with: git push --no-verify" >&2
  say_refused test-auditors "" "$tarc" "$talog"
  exit 1
fi
rm -f "$refs"

echo "" >&2
if [ "$rc" -eq 3 ]; then
  # NAME THE CAUSE THE GATE ACTUALLY REPORTED (2026-09-12). This block said "that means gate discovery is
  # broken" for every 3, and by 2026-09-11 a 3 had at least three causes: discovery collapsing, the machine-wide
  # gate worker queue not moving for 20 minutes, and a push the remote will already reject. That afternoon a
  # standalone run-gates and then a push both waited about 20 minutes for one of the 10 slots while roughly 30
  # other run-gates processes queued and the box sat near 87%, and the hook told the pusher their discovery was
  # broken. A refusal that points at the wrong cause sends somebody to debug a walk that is fine, and the next
  # thing they reach for is --no-verify - so it must name what run-gates said, or say plainly that it could not
  # tell. THE REFUSAL IS UNCONDITIONAL EITHER WAY: every branch below falls through to the same exit 1.
  #
  # TWO READS, MARKER FIRST. Exit-Guard writes "RUN-GATES-COMPLETE blind=<token>" as the last line and the
  # token travels with the exit, so it is the reliable one. Two exit-3 paths carry no token - the pool-size
  # mismatch writes no marker at all, and the no-verdict path's marker counts suites - so the COULD NOT
  # EVALUATE line is the fallback, and a 3 with neither is reported as exactly that.
  blind="$(grep -o 'RUN-GATES-COMPLETE blind=[A-Za-z0-9_-]*' "$log" 2>/dev/null | tail -n 1 | sed 's/.*blind=//')"
  reason="$(grep -E '^run-gates: COULD NOT EVALUATE' "$log" 2>/dev/null | tail -n 1)"
  echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - run-gates COULD NOT EVALUATE (exit 3). A 3 is NEVER a pass." >&2
  case "$blind" in
    no-gate-worker-slot)
      # THE BUDGET IS READ, NOT RESTATED (2026-09-18, backlog I237). This line said "The 10" for six days after
      # 39be9900e raised the budget to 24. It now reads lib/gate-slots.ps1's own assignment, and says no number
      # rather than a wrong one when it cannot.
      slots="$(grep -oE '^\$script:TcGateSlotTotal = [0-9]+' "$repo/lib/gate-slots.ps1" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1 | grep -oE '[0-9]+$')"
      echo "          CAUSE: no gate worker slot. The ${slots:+$slots }machine-wide slots stayed held by other gate runs and" >&2
      echo "          the queue did not move, so NOTHING was run. That is CONTENTION on this box, not a broken" >&2
      echo "          tree and nothing wrong with this checkout. Let the other runs drain and push again; the" >&2
      echo "          gate's own line below says how long it waited and how many runs were ahead of it." >&2
      ;;
    push-cannot-land)
      echo "          CAUSE: this push can no longer land - the remote moved while it waited, so no gate was run" >&2
      echo "          for it. Fetch, rebase onto what the remote holds now, and push again." >&2
      ;;
    no-selftests|selftest-discovery-collapsed)
      echo "          CAUSE: gate DISCOVERY is broken. run-gates found no self-tests, or far fewer than this tree" >&2
      echo "          holds, so it refused to judge. Fix the walk; a tree that cannot be examined is not a clean one." >&2
      ;;
    static-scanned-zero)
      # W6.9 (2026-09-23): ops/audit-readjson-inline-wrap.ps1 read zero files from every worktree for two days and
      # run-gates scored it ok. The token carries no gate name; run-gates' own COULD NOT EVALUATE line below does.
      echo "          CAUSE: a static gate exited 0 while its own marker said it READ NOTHING (scanned=, files=," >&2
      echo "          examined= or resolved= at 0), so it judged nothing. That is usually a walk that excludes on the" >&2
      echo "          FULL path and so drops every file in a worktree. The gate's own line below names which. Fix" >&2
      echo "          its walk; zero_ok = \$true on its run-gates entry is only for a gate whose empty set is a real answer." >&2
      ;;
    ?*)
      echo "          CAUSE: the gate reported blind=$blind. Read its own line below before doing anything else." >&2
      ;;
    *)
      if [ -n "$reason" ]; then
        echo "          The gate named no blind= cause, so its own COULD NOT EVALUATE line below is the whole" >&2
        echo "          account. Read it rather than assuming which of the causes this was." >&2
      else
        echo "          The gate said nothing about WHY, so this refusal cannot name a cause. Read the full output" >&2
        echo "          below and let it say which cause this was; every guess here has been wrong at least once." >&2
      fi
      ;;
  esac
else
  echo "pre-push: BLOCKED - run-gates exited $rc. This tree must not be pushed until it passes." >&2
  echo "          Fix the cause, never the gate." >&2
fi
grep -E "^  FAIL|^run-gates: (FAILED|COULD NOT)" "$log" 2>/dev/null | head -12 >&2
echo "          full gate output kept at: $log" >&2
echo "          Bypass with: git push --no-verify" >&2
# gate= is the first token after FAIL on run-gates' first "  FAIL" line, and is left off when there is none: a 3 from a
# queue that never moved ran nothing and failed no gate. -n and p print only a line the pattern matched.
failgate="$(grep -E '^  FAIL' "$log" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1 | sed -n -E 's/^  FAIL[[:space:]]+([^[:space:]]+).*/\1/p')"
# $blind is set only on a 3 above; on a 1 it is empty and the row records no blind token.
say_refused run-gates "$failgate" "$rc" "$log" "$blind"
exit 1
